#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069746} :activity/id #uuid "211f43a9-ed58-52eb-922b-5024799258b3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050159 :topic/id #uuid "32cf9e59-1dd8-51df-b1af-51ac52d78285" :topic/math-academy-id 415} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069988 :tutorial/content "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve $x=f(y),$ the $y$-axis, and the horizontal lines $y=c$ and $y=d,$ as shown below. If we rotate this region $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we get a solid of revolution.\n\n![](images/19/1927064dcef318f2c0490ff071532b024da30a328b2752c28e197171eeec6b5f.png)\n\nOne way of computing the volume of the solid is to divide the interval from $y=c$ to $y=d$ on the $y$-axis to get horizontal slices. This approach is called the **shell method**.\n\nConsider one such slice of height $\\Delta{y},$ as shown in the picture below (on the left). When this rectangle is rotated about the $x$-axis, it forms a solid **shell** (i.e., the skin of a cylinder).\n\n![](images/25/2561448ab909594dda427b76860fcdda5147f8966fec9e20958d1c5fca11b536.png)\n\nSince we assume that $\\Delta{y}$ is very small, the outer radius of the shell approximately equals the inner radius $y.$ The thickness of the shell is $\\Delta{y}$ and its height is $x=f(y)$.\n\nImagine we cut this shell and unfold it to obtain a rectangular solid, as shown below. Its width is $x=f(y)$, its thickness is $\\Delta{y},$ and its height is $2\\pi y$ (the circumference of the shell).\n\n![](images/03/03030fb80f0aa51c637e6d39b4f763e8d7fc89abd111a012d3e560a952cfba99.png)\n\nThe volume of the shell approximately equals the volume of the rectangular solid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{shell}} & ≈{V}_{\\text{rec}} \\\\  & =2\\pi y\\cdot x\\cdot Δy \\\\  & =2\\pi yf(y)Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum over all the shells will approximate the total volume that we want. This approximation becomes more accurate as $\\Delta{y} \\to 0$. Therefore, the volume $V$ of the solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yx\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y\\,f(y)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6b39e027-cd9c-572b-9d98-e3b708e1b618" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5286 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "72547125-fabd-5779-a865-3e7942bbad99" :step/math-academy-id 643617 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068657 :question/id #uuid "29e835eb-531e-5241-911f-45e2d686b183" :question/math-academy-id "e-8853"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "623f9081-96b8-53f5-8881-b17bea5d0316" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571419}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"} :step/id #uuid "7a174fca-da25-58a3-b88f-d062ddd5cad6" :step/math-academy-id 643622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069989 :tutorial/content "One of the advantages of using the shell method versus other methods is that the resulting integral can be much simpler to evaluate. This is often true when we want to rotate a curve of the form $x = f(y)$ about the $x$-axis.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the volume generated when the region below is rotated about the $x$-axis:\n\n![](images/1d/1d23dde2cdd557feb50f82f72f4ff09174d5fb9a0c94e7810a6c06acb95a13d0.png)\n\nAccording to the shell method, the volume $V$ is given by the following straightforward integral: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2y-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now compare this to the disc method.\n\nTo determine this volume using the disc method, we'd first need to express our curve as two separate functions of $x,$ one for the lower part and one for the upper part, as follows:\n\n![](images/d9/d957a8e263db8ddd8ad3e5fa37f63b0cdbb47e706c367350c3ff6b8ff90294ff.svg)\n\n![](images/f4/f416ab43297265aec4836eca2020d8f08976852cc025ad4d3a0766023187adef.png)\n\nThe required volume is equal to the difference between the volumes generated when the upper and lower curves are rotated about the $x$-axis. Thus, according to the disc method, the required volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{0}^{1/2} \\left( \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2x} \\right]^2 - \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2x} \\right]^2 \\right) \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAs we can see, this integral is a lot more complicated than the one we obtained using the shell method!" :tutorial/id #uuid "cb718bb7-7c09-555f-bd37-dadebd89ba90" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5292 :tutorial/title "Advantages of the Shell Method"} :step/id #uuid "4660fad3-185d-5d29-946d-ac581eb8d39f" :step/math-academy-id 643621 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068816 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068658 :question/id #uuid "77659797-1ecc-567e-b864-cfb39c21b0c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8856"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49361606-6120-5b9c-888f-0cdaf012f125" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571430}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid"} :step/id #uuid "b93bd632-6fba-5317-ad2d-eb331be9e45c" :step/math-academy-id 643618 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069748}}] :activity/title "The Shell Method: Rotating a Region About the X-Axis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068950}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571493} :activity/id #uuid "6a557ca9-7a93-510a-b204-bd1296cf335a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050159 :topic/id #uuid "32cf9e59-1dd8-51df-b1af-51ac52d78285" :topic/math-academy-id 415} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba3a988-67bf-5829-9981-f9cf880f3bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570981} {:answer/id #uuid "cbad17f3-9c38-537a-bdd3-f2e9cbdf9adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571204} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3700c5-3cd8-53dd-ad0e-aa6f7f89811e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571205} {:answer/id #uuid "ccecad03-8fcd-507c-9d96-5c67bc77b34c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571207} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2f37a3-ca1f-5389-9d5a-ce4762cbcf9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571276}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571204} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecddd1b8-00da-5bd7-a5d9-f8af1c70264b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4f112823-f777-5f74-ab7d-687fcdb73828" :question/math-academy-id "q-105740" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=5{y}^{3}-5{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{3}-5{y}^{4} & =0 \\\\ 5{y}^{3}(1-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(5{y}^{3}-5{y}^{4})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =10\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{4}-{y}^{5})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =10\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{5}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =10\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{6}}{6}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =10\\pi ((\\frac{1}{5}-0)-(\\frac{1}{6}-0)) \\\\  & =10\\pi (\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8d03fea6-c5c4-5d31-b587-b42678eb1cd0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571520}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67cd5b99-2c08-5337-96e7-18e6ea444e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{(3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571051} {:answer/id #uuid "a0817336-df3b-51ab-9ac0-700055776676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{(3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571140} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5dea82-8e37-5384-b0e3-ed2d52489c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}y(3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571159} {:answer/id #uuid "b12bb577-b2fe-5bd1-84d4-8ff76cc692d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}y(3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571165} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e3fabf-7ba9-566c-ae98-17b03c9b1228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571226}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571165} :answer-field/id #uuid "edb95141-3ad9-54ec-a8b4-67b0e62cf950" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9e08ef03-042b-5ef4-bd8e-13473edc79ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-105677" :question/problem "![](images/af/af0f21f914c1a773cf80ba80a43547a382a1bf4007b1916cca8ce90f6b2d15b2.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(3-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0,\\,3.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=3.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}y(3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8e96fcaf-3825-5afc-b540-f2f106da0fb4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571508}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571499 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2063900e-a5bb-505d-a56c-ffbb8015f598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{6\\,250}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570937} {:answer/id #uuid "25ba7a39-0faa-584a-b636-d552b75ef30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6\\,250}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570948} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b348ee-b44b-559b-ab92-6aec9c390e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{6\\,250}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571143} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd3c117-dfc5-5139-9f10-1f99cce8bfa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{3\\,125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571180} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbb14af-13a9-556e-b543-a0172a174ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3\\,125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570948} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a83f496-fbd8-5701-a047-62d0e562d2fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8025abae-d2ce-537b-9664-a4fb5d59e410" :question/math-academy-id "q-126114" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x={y}^{2}-5{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-5{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(1-5y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{5}.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{1}{5}.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/5}y({y}^{2}-5{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/5}({y}^{3}-5{y}^{4})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1/5}-5\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}|}_{0}^{1/5}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{4\\cdot {5}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{5}^{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{{5}^{4}}(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{20\\cdot {5}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\,250}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c08bf546-3c9a-5b7a-b09e-fceefa06f284"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571508 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22968325-f0bc-5a5e-afd8-7899db95af90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{(2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570940} {:answer/id #uuid "53062cc0-a982-5374-a7a2-4902d13970e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571017} {:answer/id #uuid "5bba9e90-64fa-5409-8ec7-36b4fabd173f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571036} {:answer/id #uuid "954a8d5d-1191-5868-9419-c6d80134e700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571124} {:answer/id #uuid "e773a89d-eef5-5433-b270-7313a2f284de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y(2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571124} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9256cfa-3a8e-5a21-89fd-ea3ec6a4be6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7b1c2b26-9aae-5367-9691-64e9ca75fb9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105679" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}(2-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0,\\,2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d6e843e1-379e-5dab-ac11-b1d60046f2d5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571492}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050b1a2d-390a-5cca-ba66-abe1a81fc0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}y(-8+6y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570899} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3ccc49-8996-5d4a-bf7f-39d68a647521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}y(-8+6y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571081} {:answer/id #uuid "95e473b2-f5c4-5b02-ae5a-5c17d81d6906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}{(-8+6y-{y}^{2})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571127} {:answer/id #uuid "e4502299-a253-55a2-aafe-cef6f38bdf90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}(-8+6y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571246} {:answer/id #uuid "f670e967-be71-5966-bb43-e648c3783902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{(-8+6y-{y}^{2})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571081} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d3ed104-ec6c-5ae1-a397-51e099e513ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0342016a-166b-5391-a2e8-8b20bd450fa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-126104" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=-8+6y-{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8+6y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-6y+8 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)(y-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=2$ and $y=4.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=2$ to $y=4.$\n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}y(-8+6y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ff168382-b5a6-589b-a738-530a2fb9b146" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571499}}] :activity/title "The Shell Method: Rotating a Region About the X-Axis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570883}]}
